0336: SapphireGpu, SweetFramebuffer: read-only diagnostics of the GPU driver and the presentation path

Counters, times and histograms behind two read-only sysctls; nothing changes what is submitted, copied or
presented, except with kern.sweet_gpu_marks=1 (default 0). Both sysctls are cumulative: read twice and
subtract.

kern.sweet_fb_diag: SwapEnd-to-SwapEnd intervals; intervals between flips counted in the panel's own
refreshes (INTF_FRAME_COUNT read at each flip); SwapEnd-to-flip latency; frames whose copy was replaced in
the back buffer before any flip ("overwritten"); vsync timer lateness; the eighth of the refresh in which
flips are written (INTF_LINE_COUNT); time in SwapEnd's present; SwapWait calls; and one more line every
5 s beside "SWEET FB: 5 s".

kern.sweet_gpu_diag: GEM_SUBMIT (count, command buffers, buffers named; time in the call, waiting for and
holding service_lock, and in dc civac of surfaces, with bytes; ring fill); GEM_NEW by size class with the
time to zero and to map; GEM_CLOSE, frees, IOSurface imports; SMMU TLB flushes and their time; fence waits;
panel copies.

kern.sweet_gpu_marks=1: each msm submission and each panel copy is put between two CP_REG_TO_MEM of
CP_ALWAYS_ON_COUNTER (19.2 MHz), as Linux's a6xx_submit does for its stats: the GPU's own time for each,
with histograms (8 more dwords in the ring per submission or copy).

Measured with it on the phone (one boot, ~15 min of use): 5520 submissions spent 8.91 s in GEM_SUBMIT, of
which 8.64 s in cache maintenance (28.0 GB in 5675 passes, ~1.5 ms and ~4.9 MB per submission, under the
ring's lock); 931 of 6758 swapped frames (13.8 %) were overwritten before reaching the panel; 13358 TLB
invalidations took 0.97 s, 13251 of them slept. With kern.sweet_gpu_marks=1 (one window of 1268
submissions): 8.3 ms of GPU time per submission and 4.15 ms per panel copy on average.

